.TH VARCOD 8 "2026-11-03" "varco 1.0.3" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
varcod \- Varco interactive per-application firewall service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B varcod
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B varcod
loads an nftables table (\fIinet varco\fR) that sends every new
connection, outgoing or incoming, to an NFQUEUE. For each one it identifies
the program (executable, command line, user, systemd unit) and the remote
side (the name the system really resolved, and optionally whois), then
accepts it if a rule allows it, drops it if a rule denies it, or holds it
until the user decides. Unanswered connections are blocked after
\fIdecision_timeout\fR seconds.
.PP
The service is fail-closed. Before the daemon starts, systemd loads
\fI/usr/share/varco/failsafe.nft\fR, so new connections are dropped
even if the daemon cannot start. If the daemon dies or hangs, the queue has
no reader and the kernel drops new connections; systemd restarts it
(\fBRestart=always\fR, \fBWatchdogSec=30\fR). The table is checked every
5 seconds and loaded again if another program removed or changed it (for
example \fBnft flush ruleset\fR). Turning protection off from the interface
or removing the package deletes the table and frees the network.
.PP
The kill switch drops all traffic on input, output and forward hooks on
every interface and survives restarts. A pause ends when the computer
restarts.
.PP
Every request carries the network interface and its kind (Wi-Fi, mobile
broadband, Ethernet, VPN); rules may be limited to one kind.
.PP
It also watches program starts through the kernel proc connector and
reports programs never seen before and programs with suspicious traits.
These reports are heuristics, not proof of malware.
.PP
The service is controlled over the D-Bus system bus
(\fIorg.k1m1k.Varco1\fR). Authorization uses the polkit actions
\fIorg.k1m1k.varco.monitor\fR, \fIdecide\fR and \fImanage\fR.
.PP
.B systemctl reload varco
(or SIGHUP) reloads configuration and rules without stopping the filter.
.PP
The service is not enabled at installation: turn it on from
.BR varco-gtk (1)
or with
.BR "systemctl enable \-\-now varco" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/varco/config.json
Default settings (configuration file).
.TP
.I /var/lib/varco/settings.json
Settings changed from the interfaces; they override the defaults.
.TP
.I /var/lib/varco/rules.json
Permanent rules (format version 2). Rules that are not valid are never applied
and are kept under \fIinvalid\fR. A file that cannot be read is renamed to
\fIrules.json.damaged\-DATE\fR instead of being overwritten.
.TP
.I /var/lib/varco/state.json
Kill switch and pause state, kept across restarts.
.TP
.I /var/lib/varco/known-programs.json
Programs already seen by the watchdog.
.SH NOTES
Traffic forwarded to containers or virtual machines is not filtered. A
program that already runs as root can stop any firewall, this one included.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR varco (1),
.BR varco-gtk (1),
.BR nft (8)
